.TH Hearts 3
.XI Hearts - "\*(mS version of the card game Hearts"
.SH NAME
Hearts \- \*(mS version of the card game Hearts
.Me $a
.SH SYNOPSIS
.na
\fBHearts\fR
.ad
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBHearts\fR  is a  trick-taking  card game for four  players,  played  with a
standard  pack of 52 cards. The user plays the south hand  against three
computer  opponents  named West, North and East. The object of the game is to
score as few points as possible.
.PP
Each  player is dealt 13 cards. Before play begins each player passes 3 cards
to another  player, and receives 3 in return. The queen of spades may never be
passed. The three cards received are held up out of the hand for a moment
before they settle into it, so that they can be seen.  The player to the  dealer's  left leads the first  trick and may lead
any card. Each player in turn must follow the suit that was led if they are able,
otherwise  they may play any card. The  highest  card of the suit led wins the
trick, and the winner leads the next one.
.PP
By  default  there  are no  restrictions  on what may be led or  played \- any
card may be led to the first trick, hearts may be led at any time and points may
be taken on the very first trick. Each of the three traditional restrictions may
be turned on individually from the options page.
.SH SCORING
Under the default house scoring a hand is worth 33 points:
.IP "\fBQueen of spades\fR"
10 points
.IP "\fBAce of hearts\fR"
5 points
.IP "\fBKing of hearts\fR"
4 points
.IP "\fBQueen of hearts\fR"
3 points
.IP "\fBJack of hearts\fR"
2 points
.IP "\fBTwo to ten of hearts\fR"
1 point each
.PP
The  classic  scoring, in which  every  heart is worth 1 point and the queen of
spades is worth 13 for a total of 26, may be selected from the options page.
.PP
If a single player takes  every  point in a hand they have  \fBshot the moon\fR.
That player scores nothing and every other player is given the full value of the
hand instead.
.PP
The game ends when any player  reaches  the target  score, 100 points by
default. The player with the lowest score at that point is the winner.
.SH CONTROLS
The game may be played with the mouse or the keyboard. To play a card, or to
select a card to be passed, click on it with the left mouse button. Cards
selected for passing are raised out of the hand.
.PP
Using the keyboard:
.IP "\fIleft\fR and \fIright\fR"
Move the highlight along the hand. The highlight wraps round at either end, so
the quickest way to a card on the far side of the hand is often to run off the
near edge.
.IP "\fIspace\fR or \fIreturn\fR"
Play the highlighted card, or select it to be passed.
.IP "\fId\fR"
Deal the next hand.
.IP "\fIp\fR"
Pass the selected cards.
.IP "\fIc\fR"
Clear the current pass selection.
.IP "\fBContinue\fR"
While the cards just received are on show, any key or click puts them into the
hand and starts play without waiting for the pause to run out.
.IP "\fIo\fR"
Show the options page.
.IP "\fIs\fR"
Save the options and leave the options page.
.IP "\fIx\fR"
Quit the game. On the options page it cancels instead, so the game cannot be
left by accident from there.
.IP "\fIesc h\fR"
This help page.
.PP
To the right of the board are the control  buttons.  These are shown only when
they may be used, so \fBDeal\fR and \fBOptions\fR appear between hands, and
\fBPass\fR and \fBClear\fR while cards are being passed.
.PP
Note that the screen may be updated at any time using "\fIC-l\fR".
.SH OPTIONS
The options page is reached with the \fBOptions\fR button between hands. Press
\fI1\fR to \fI9\fR to change a setting, then \fBSave\fR ("\fIs\fR") to keep the
changes  and  remember  them  between  sessions,  or  \fBCancel\fR ("\fIc\fR")
to put everything back as it was. Saved changes take effect from the next hand
dealt.
.IP "\fBDifficulty\fR"
Five levels from \fBBeginner\fR to \fBExpert\fR. The lower three play by
judgement alone; the upper two also search, playing the hand out many times
over against guessed  distributions of the cards they cannot see before
choosing. The weaker levels
are weaker  because they miss things  \- they do not always find the best card,
and they do not keep track of who has shown out of a suit \- not because they
play at random.
.IP
Each level was measured against the one below it over several thousand hands, so
every step up is a real increase in strength rather than simply a slower one.
.IP "\fBPassing\fR"
Pass left every hand, or rotate left, right and across. There is no hold hand.
.IP "\fBScoring\fR"
House scoring for a 33 point hand, or classic scoring for a 26 point hand.
.IP "\fBJack of Diamonds\fR"
The Omnibus  Hearts  variant, off by default. When  enabled the jack of diamonds
is worth -10  points to  whoever  captures  it. It is not needed to shoot the
moon, and the bonus still applies if the player shooting the moon takes it.
.IP "\fBFirst lead\fR"
When enabled the holder of the two of clubs must lead it to the first trick.
Off by default, in which case the player to the dealer's left leads any card.
.IP "\fBLeading hearts\fR"
When enabled a heart may not be led until a heart has been played to some
earlier trick, unless the hand holds nothing else. Off by default.
.IP "\fBFirst trick\fR"
When enabled no card carrying points may be played to the first trick unless
there is no alternative. Off by default.
.IP "\fBGame ends at\fR"
The target score, 50, 100 or 200 points.
.IP "\fBPlay speed\fR"
How quickly the computer players play their cards.
.SH NOTES
\fBHearts\fR is a macro defined in \fChearts.emf\fR.
.PP
The computer players use no information that is not available to the user. They
see their own cards, the cards that have been played, the points taken and which
players have shown out of a suit \- all of it on the table for anyone to follow.
At the higher levels they also count how many cards each player still holds, and
deal the unseen cards out at random among them to search; the identity of those
cards is never consulted.
